<Guide slug="murungai-moringa-honey">
  <aside class="g-note" aria-label="In short">
    <p class="eyebrow">In short</p>
    <p>Murungai honey is made by bees from the flowers of the drumstick tree, also called moringa. It is usually light amber to amber, thick and smooth, with a mild sweetness and a light herbal note. It is a gentle everyday honey, and it tends to taste more alike from one harvest to the next than multifloral honey, which changes with every season’s flowers.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>What is murungai honey?</h2>
  <p>Murungai is the Tamil name for the drumstick tree, the moringa, known for its long green pods and its small, feathery leaves, both of which go into the cooking pot. When the tree flowers, it covers itself in clusters of small, cream-white blossoms, and bees visit them for nectar and pollen. Honey made mostly from that nectar is called murungai honey, or moringa honey.</p>
  <p>It is a single-flower, or monofloral, honey. That does not mean the bees visited nothing else. Bees fly where they please, and no honey comes from one flower alone. It means the drumstick blossom was the main source while the honey was being made, so its character comes through in the jar. A beekeeper gets a single-flower honey through timing: the honey is taken from the hives when one bloom is the main thing flowering around them.</p>

  <GuideFigure src="murungai-pods.jpg" alt="Drumstick pods, split seed pods, cream-white moringa flowers and small green leaves" caption="The drumstick tree gives pods, leaves and, for the bees, small cream-white flowers." />

  <h2>What does murungai honey taste like?</h2>
  <p>Mild is the word most people use. The sweetness is soft rather than sharp, and there is a light herbal note in the finish. It is not strong and woody like dark forest honey, and it does not have the bright, perfumed lift of some fruit-blossom honeys. That gentleness is why it suits people who find strong honey too much.</p>
  <p>Like every natural honey, the taste changes a little from harvest to harvest, with the weather, the season and whatever else was flowering nearby. Taste two batches side by side and you may notice small differences. That is normal, and a sign of a honey that has not been blended to taste the same every time.</p>

  <h2>Colour and texture</h2>
  <p>Murungai honey usually sits between light amber and amber on the honey colour scale: a warm golden colour when you hold the jar up to the light. Ours is thick and smooth, and it falls from a spoon in a slow ribbon.</p>
  <p>Colour depends on the flowers, the season and the age of the honey, so one batch can be a shade lighter or darker than the last. Raw murungai honey may also turn cloudy or set thick in cool weather. That is crystallisation, and it is a natural thing for real honey to do. Our guide on <a href={guideHref('how-to-check-pure-honey')}>how to tell if honey is pure</a> explains why it happens and how to make set honey runny again.</p>

  <h2>When is murungai honey harvested?</h2>
  <p>Drumstick trees flower in particular seasons rather than all year round, and the bees can only make this honey while the trees are in bloom. So murungai honey comes in limited batches, and it can run out until the next flowering. You can see the usual flowering months for our honeys on the <a href={link('/harvest/#bloom-calendar')}>bloom calendar</a>.</p>
  <p>Every Beezy pack carries a batch number. Type it into <a href={link('/know-your-jar/')}>Know Your Jar</a> to see when that honey was harvested and packed.</p>

  <h2>How to use murungai honey</h2>
  <p>Because the flavour is gentle, murungai honey goes with almost anything. A few easy ideas:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Stir a spoonful into a glass of warm water in the morning, with a squeeze of lemon if you like.</li>
    <li>Drizzle it over hot dosa, chapati or toast.</li>
    <li>Spoon it over curd, fresh fruit or a bowl of oats.</li>
    <li>Sweeten tea, coffee or milk once the drink has cooled a little.</li>
    <li>Whisk it into a salad dressing with lemon juice, oil, salt and pepper.</li>
    <li>Eat the comb. Our murungai honey comes with a piece of honeycomb in the box, and the wax is edible. Our guide to <a href={guideHref('honey-with-comb')}>honey with comb</a> shows how to cut it and enjoy it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>One tip: very hot liquids flatten the delicate taste of any honey, so let tea or milk cool to sipping temperature before you stir it in. Always use a dry spoon, and close the lid after use so moisture stays out.</p>

  <h2>Murungai honey vs multifloral honey</h2>
  <p>Multifloral honey is made when bees gather nectar from many kinds of flowers in the same season: wildflowers, fruit trees and field crops. Murungai honey comes mainly from one flower, the drumstick blossom. That one difference shapes everything else.</p>
  <div class="g-table">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col"><span class="sr-only">Compared</span></th><th scope="col">Murungai honey</th><th scope="col">Multifloral honey</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Nectar</th><td>Mainly drumstick tree flowers</td><td>Many flowers in one season</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Taste</th><td>Mild, with a light herbal note</td><td>Balanced and floral</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Harvest to harvest</th><td>Fairly alike</td><td>Changes with the season’s flowers</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">When</th><td>Only when drumstick trees flower</td><td>Across more of the year</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Good for</th><td>Tasting one flower; a gentle, steady flavour</td><td>An everyday honey for the whole family</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Neither is better. <a href={productHref('multifloral-honey')}>Multifloral honey</a> is a lovely everyday honey because it captures a whole season of flowers. <a href={productHref('murungai-honey')}>Murungai honey</a> is for people who want to taste one flower. Many families keep both: multifloral for daily drinks and cooking, and murungai for the moments when the honey itself is the point.</p>

  <h2>Murungai honey benefits: an honest answer</h2>
  <p>Search for moringa honey and you will find long lists of promises. We do not make them. Moringa leaves and pods are well known in their own right, but bees make honey from the flower’s nectar, not from the leaves, so the honey does not simply carry over what people say about the rest of the tree.</p>
  <p>What we can say plainly is this: murungai honey is a raw, unheated food with a mild flavour, made by bees from a tree many families grow at home. Enjoy it for its taste and use it where you would use any honey. If you have a question about your health, a doctor is the right person to ask, not a honey label.</p>

  <h2>Buying murungai honey</h2>
  <p>A good single-flower honey should come with clear information: which flower it is from, when it was harvested, and a batch number you can ask about. Our <a href={productHref('murungai-honey')}>Murungai Honey</a> is packed raw, with a piece of real honeycomb, in a clear box so you can see the comb before you open it. Not sure which honey suits you? The <a href={link('/honey-finder/')}>Honey Finder</a> asks four quick questions and suggests one.</p>
</Guide>
